<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>io.github.gor3pig.Custicon</id>
  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>GPL-3.0-or-later</project_license>

  <name translate="no">Custicon</name>
  <summary>Customize the icon of a folder in your file manager</summary>
  <description>
    <p>
      Custicon adds a pictogram to the icon of a folder, like the Documents or Music folders have.
      The pictogram is placed on the real folder icon of your current icon theme, so the result
      follows your theme.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Choose among thousands of pictograms: your icon theme, Phosphor, emoji and brand logos, your own SVG or PNG file, or a few letters</li>
      <li>Get suggestions from the name of the folder, and find your recent and favorite pictograms</li>
      <li>Pick colors from a palette, drag the pictogram on the preview and scroll to resize it</li>
      <li>Tint the folder itself, or follow the accent color of your system</li>
      <li>Customize or restore several folders at once</li>
      <li>Save named styles, and regenerate every icon after a change of icon theme</li>
      <li>Remove an icon at any time: the folder gets back the icon it had before</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Custicon works with GNOME Files (Nautilus), Nemo, Caja, Dolphin and PCManFM-Qt. Thunar (Xfce),
      PCManFM (LXDE) and COSMIC Files cannot show custom folder icons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Custicon needs access to your home folder to know where your folders are, and it has no access to
      the network. It never changes the content of your folders: Dolphin and PCManFM-Qt keep the icon in a
      small hidden .directory file inside the folder, which Custicon removes when you remove the icon.
    </p>
  </description>

